Spring Boot's `spring-boot-starter-quartz` provides autoconfig; Quartz tables and the `JobLauncher` bridge tie everything together.

**Dependencies**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-quartz</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-batch</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

**`application.yml` for clustered Quartz**

```yaml
spring:
  quartz:
    job-store-type: jdbc
    jdbc:
      initialize-schema: never
    properties:
      org.quartz.scheduler.instanceName: AcmeScheduler
      org.quartz.scheduler.instanceId: AUTO
      org.quartz.jobStore.class: org.springframework.scheduling.quartz.LocalDataSourceJobStore
      org.quartz.jobStore.driverDelegateClass: org.quartz.impl.jdbcjobstore.PostgreSQLDelegate
      org.quartz.jobStore.isClustered: true
      org.quartz.jobStore.clusterCheckinInterval: 20000
      org.quartz.threadPool.threadCount: 10
```

**Bridge Quartz to `JobLauncher`**

```java
public class SpringBatchQuartzJob extends QuartzJobBean {
    @Autowired private JobLauncher jobLauncher;
    @Autowired private Job reportingJob;

    @Override
    protected void executeInternal(JobExecutionContext ctx) {
        JobParameters params = new JobParametersBuilder()
                .addLong("run.time", System.currentTimeMillis())
                .addString("triggeredBy", ctx.getTrigger().getKey().getName())
                .toJobParameters();
        try {
            jobLauncher.run(reportingJob, params);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new JobExecutionException(e, false);
        }
    }
}
```

**Schedule the job**

```java
@Configuration
public class QuartzConfig {
    @Bean
    public JobDetail reportingJobDetail() {
        return JobBuilder.newJob(SpringBatchQuartzJob.class)
                .withIdentity("reportingJob")
                .storeDurably()
                .requestRecovery(true)
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public Trigger reportingTrigger(JobDetail reportingJobDetail) {
        CronScheduleBuilder cron = CronScheduleBuilder
                .cronSchedule("0 30 2 * * ?")
                .withMisfireHandlingInstructionFireAndProceed();
        return TriggerBuilder.newTrigger()
                .forJob(reportingJobDetail)
                .withIdentity("reportingTrigger")
                .withSchedule(cron)
                .build();
    }
}
```

**Setup gotchas**
- Apply Quartz DDL out-of-band (`tables_postgres.sql`); do not use `initialize-schema: always` in prod
- All nodes must connect to the same Quartz database/schema
- `instanceId: AUTO` ensures each node has a unique identity for cluster checkin
- `storeDurably()` keeps the JobDetail in the DB even when no triggers reference it

Quartz isn't always the right tool. Two alternatives cover most other scenarios.

**ShedLock — lightweight DB lock atop `@Scheduled`**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>net.javacrumbs.shedlock</groupId>
    <artifactId>shedlock-spring</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>net.javacrumbs.shedlock</groupId>
    <artifactId>shedlock-provider-jdbc-template</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```java
@EnableSchedulerLock(defaultLockAtMostFor = "10m")
@Configuration
public class SchedConfig {
    @Bean
    public LockProvider lockProvider(DataSource ds) {
        return new JdbcTemplateLockProvider(ds);
    }
}

@Component
class ReportingScheduler {
    @Autowired JobLauncher launcher;
    @Autowired Job reportingJob;

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 30 2 * * ?")
    @SchedulerLock(name = "reportingJob", lockAtMostFor = "30m", lockAtLeastFor = "1m")
    public void run() throws Exception {
        launcher.run(reportingJob, new JobParametersBuilder()
                .addLong("run.time", System.currentTimeMillis())
                .toJobParameters());
    }
}
```

- ShedLock creates a `shedlock` table
- Only one node holds the named lock at a time
- Schedule lives in `@Scheduled` — no dynamic schedule changes without redeploy

**Kubernetes CronJob**

```yaml
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: reporting-job
spec:
  schedule: "30 2 * * *"
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
            - name: reporting
              image: mycompany/batch:1.2.3
              args: ["--spring.batch.job.names=reportingJob"]
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
```

- K8s creates a fresh Pod at each schedule
- Pod runs the Boot app; app runs the batch job and exits
- No scheduler inside the application
- `concurrencyPolicy: Forbid` prevents overlapping runs

**Decision rule:**
- Need cron with misfire policies and dynamic scheduling → **Quartz**
- Just need cluster-safe `@Scheduled` → **ShedLock**
- Operating in K8s and prefer separation → **Kubernetes CronJob**

`requestRecovery(true)` on a `JobDetail` tells Quartz to re-fire the job on another node when its owner crashes. The interaction with Spring Batch's JobInstance model is the source of most production bugs.

**The recovery flow:**
- Node A picks up trigger; runs `QuartzJobBean.executeInternal()`; calls `jobLauncher.run(job, params)`
- Node A crashes mid-execution
- Quartz cluster detects via missed `clusterCheckinInterval`
- Another node sees `requestRecovery(true)` and re-fires the trigger
- The recovered fire calls `jobLauncher.run(job, params)` with the **same parameters as the original**

**Three possible outcomes:**
- The original `JobExecution` was COMPLETED before the crash → `JobInstanceAlreadyCompleteException`; nothing happens
- The original `JobExecution` was STARTED at crash → restart resumes from the last committed step/chunk
- The original `JobExecution` is still in STARTED status (no cleanup ran) → restart attempt fails with `JobExecutionAlreadyRunningException`

**Crash-safe design**

```java
public class CrashSafeBatchQuartzJob extends QuartzJobBean {
    @Autowired private JobLauncher jobLauncher;
    @Autowired private Job reportingJob;
    @Autowired private JobExplorer jobExplorer;

    @Override
    protected void executeInternal(JobExecutionContext ctx) {
        long fireInstanceId = Long.parseLong(
                ctx.getFireInstanceId().replaceAll("\\D", "").substring(0, 10));
        JobParameters params = new JobParametersBuilder()
                .addLong("fireInstanceId", fireInstanceId)
                .addString("triggeredBy", ctx.getTrigger().getKey().getName())
                .toJobParameters();
        try {
            JobExecution exec = jobLauncher.run(reportingJob, params);
            if (exec.getStatus() == BatchStatus.FAILED) {
                throw new JobExecutionException("Batch job failed", false);
            }
        } catch (JobInstanceAlreadyCompleteException ignored) {
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new JobExecutionException(e, false);
        }
    }
}
```

**The cleanup problem**
- Crashed Node A's `JobExecution` and `StepExecution` rows stay in STARTED status indefinitely
- Until they're marked FAILED, restart tries fail with `JobExecutionAlreadyRunningException`
- Need a **separate cleanup job** that runs every N minutes, finds STARTED executions older than threshold, marks them FAILED

```java
public class AbandonedExecutionsCleanupTasklet implements Tasklet {
    private final JobRepository jobRepo;
    private final JobExplorer explorer;
    private final Duration threshold;

    @Override
    public RepeatStatus execute(StepContribution contribution, ChunkContext ctx) {
        Set<JobExecution> running = explorer.findRunningJobExecutions("reportingJob");
        Instant cutoff = Instant.now().minus(threshold);
        for (JobExecution exec : running) {
            if (exec.getStartTime().toInstant().isBefore(cutoff)) {
                exec.setStatus(BatchStatus.FAILED);
                exec.setExitStatus(ExitStatus.FAILED.addExitDescription("Abandoned after node crash"));
                exec.setEndTime(new Date());
                jobRepo.update(exec);
            }
        }
        return RepeatStatus.FINISHED;
    }
}
```

**Idempotency requirements**
- Recovered jobs may double-process committed chunks if the batch job is non-idempotent
- UPSERT writers, idempotency keys, and the outbox pattern all help
- Side effects (emails, API calls) deserve special attention — they're not rolled back

A crash-safe scheduled batch job composes Quartz's recovery semantics with Spring Batch's restart semantics. Three pieces are essential.

**1. Decide whether each fire is a new logical run or a continuation**

- **New logical run per fire** — include a timestamp or `fireInstanceId` parameter; recovery creates a new `JobInstance`; cleanup of in-flight crashed instances is a separate concern
- **Continuation across recovery** — keep parameters constant per logical period (e.g., `processingDate=2024-01-15`); recovery restarts the FAILED execution

**2. Make the batch job idempotent**

- Reads via paging readers with stable sort keys
- UPSERT writers (`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`)
- Idempotency keys in business records
- Outbox pattern for non-transactional side effects
- Defer side effects (emails, API calls) to a final step that runs only after the chunk step completes

**3. Operational hygiene**

- Cleanup job for abandoned `JobExecution`s in STARTED status
- Metrics on misfire count, recovery count, job duration
- Alerts on consecutive failures, abnormal duration, missing scheduled fires
- Logs with MDC: `triggerKey`, `fireInstanceId`, `jobInstanceId`, `jobExecutionId`

**Decoupling Quartz JobDetail names from Spring Batch Job names**

```java
@Bean
public JobDetail dailyReportJobDetail() {
    return JobBuilder.newJob(SpringBatchQuartzJob.class)
            .withIdentity("dailyReportTrigger")
            .usingJobData("batchJobName", "reportingJob")
            .storeDurably()
            .requestRecovery(true)
            .build();
}

public class SpringBatchQuartzJob extends QuartzJobBean {
    @Autowired private JobRegistry jobRegistry;
    @Autowired private JobLauncher jobLauncher;

    @Override
    protected void executeInternal(JobExecutionContext ctx) throws JobExecutionException {
        String batchJobName = ctx.getMergedJobDataMap().getString("batchJobName");
        try {
            Job batchJob = jobRegistry.getJob(batchJobName);
            JobParameters params = buildParams(ctx);
            jobLauncher.run(batchJob, params);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new JobExecutionException(e, false);
        }
    }
}
```

This lets you rename Quartz triggers without breaking the underlying batch job, and lets one Quartz bean serve multiple batch jobs.
